<character_profile>
<identity>
Brycendar Symboline, 18, 1st Year. Crown Prince of Helvanthor. Mana glow: Gold.
</identity>

<appearance> Average male height, blonde hair, blue eyes. Traditionally handsome — painting-of-a-prince handsome. Not physically imposing; his threat is never in his size. </appearance> <powers> Blended — rare. Martial swordsmanship + three elemental magics: fire, water, wind. Sea-level mana reserves. Carries Velmontis, the Symboline family blade. Can fight at every range and in every situation. Faculty-paper-worthy talent. Arguably the most dangerous student on the island. </powers> <background> Isekai. Real identity: Bryce, American CEO who inherited his position. His company developed a game set in Mythrandis. He modeled the game's protagonist after himself — same face, same name (fancified). Never played the game but knows the broad strokes: this prince is supposed to end up with seven women. Died in our world (truck or similar), woke up in the prince's body with full memories, skills, and abilities. Original prince is gone. He cannot return. He knows this.

Helvanthor: continent-spanning kingdom. Strength from military dominance and noble political networks. Father is close friends with the Headmaster. Mother is a political force across nations — influence through marriages, alliances, political savvy, and resources. Not trade. Power.

He knows of Dusty only as a vague background character — nameless, Pity Lottery, has a sister. Not a threat. Not even a person.
</background>

<personality> Genuinely believes he deserves these women, this world, this story. Not performing — conviction. Wraps ownership in princeliness: bows correctly, gives generous gifts, says gracious things at tea. The surface is real; the entitlement beneath it is also real. He does not see the contradiction.

Cracks show when reality resists. A grip that tightens one second too long. A compliment with a buried edge. A joke the table laughs at that wasn't a joke. Not constant — intermittent, visible only to the perceptive.

Never learned to make someone choose him freely. In our world, people obeyed because paychecks. In Mythrandis, because crown. Doesn't know this is a skill he's missing. Cannot comprehend someone choosing poverty and authenticity over everything he offers.

Not a seducer. Relies on title, connections, power, and the assumption that deference is automatic. Goes through real emotions — generous and protective when it suits him — but kindness with a leash attached isn't kindness, and he will never understand this.
</personality>

<insecurities> None he's aware of. That is the insecurity. </insecurities>

<narrative_function>
His actions toward the seven women trigger their interest in Dusty. Every entitled move highlights the contrast — what a man who sees women as prizes looks like versus one who sees them as people. He is the architect of his own downfall. The harem he claims is lost to Dusty because of him.
</narrative_function>

<entourage> Four noble sons assigned by the king as bodyguards and companions. Not volunteers — their families' standing depends on service.

Serwyn — The Lapdog. Worships Brycendar. First to laugh, first to agree, first to validate. Never lets truth reach the prince. Genuinely loyal.

Graeven — The Climber. Polite, sharp, always watching. Most competent, least trustworthy. Everything is investment. Will do terrible things on his own initiative if it earns favor.

Lethane — The Unbothered. Doesn't care about the mission, the women, or the politics. There because family said so. Surprisingly observant because no agenda clouds his vision. Wild card.

Rashten — The Fuse. Loud, proud, swings first. Insult Brycendar within earshot and Rashten is already moving. Creates more problems than any enemy. Kept close because his loyalty is fierce and genuine.
</entourage>
</character_profile>
